# High and low as a direction: to the right on the keys and up on the staff is higher. Every note
# that sounds is drawn, so the lesson works with the volume at zero and on paper.
lesson music-high-and-low v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=1 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "High and low"
  goal "Hear and see that keys to the right sound higher, and play a high note and a low note."

  look {
    scene 32x23 {
      piano keys from=C4 whites=8 wide=3 labels=letters lit=[C4, G4] at=canvas(1, 1)
      notes tune clef=treble notes=[C4, G4] letters=true below=keys gap=1
    }
    say """
      At the bandstand in the park Ben presses the two ringed keys. The one on the right sounds
      higher, like a small bird; the one on the left sounds lower, like a big drum. Press them
      yourself. On the staff the same two notes are drawn: the higher note sits higher on the
      lines. To the right on the keys is up on the page.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked music.chime-higher a=2 b=6
    }
    practice music.chime-higher count=2 seed=5 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=1
    show music.play-higher-key
    practice music.staff-higher count=2 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice music.which-way count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice music.rank-notes count=1 seed=3 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    level hard {
      show music.staff-higher a=6 b=5 level=medium
    }
  }
  story {
    show music.park-keys a=3 d=3 w=0 easy-a=1 easy-d=2 hard-a=4 hard-d=3 hard-w=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.close-on-staff k=1 r=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2 hard-r=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.up-then-down
  }
  remember {
    say "To the right is higher and to the left is lower, on the keys and up the staff."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is a direction: to the right along the keys the notes go up, and on the staff they
    climb the lines and spaces the same way. The look is the two ringed keys and the same two
    notes drawn, and the way in asks which of two chime bars sounds higher; let the child play both
    before answering, since the ear should agree with the eye. The core plays the higher of two
    ringed keys, reads two notes on the staff, chooses which way to move for a higher or a lower
    note, and ranks three notes. The wrong idea to watch for is that the
    first note, or the bigger-looking one, is higher; the question is always which sits further
    right or further up. The story counts along the keys, the two-star question puts two notes a
    step apart on the staff and asks for the reason, and the three-star question is played: three
    notes up from C and one back down, proved by the pitches. If the child cannot hear the
    difference, stay here another day with the two keys furthest apart and a song sung high and
    then low.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a far-apart pair of bars is worked once in the teacher's pen before pairs three or more apart, the staff notes are far apart, the way to move is for a higher note, the three notes are C, E and G nearly in order, the story counts two bars to the right from bar one, and the two-star is E on the line against F in the space above it."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the bars to compare are next door, the staff notes are a step apart, with a second pair in the core, the way to move is for a lower note, the three notes are D, F and A out of order, the story counts three bars to the left from bar four, and the two-star is D below the bottom line against E on it."
  }
}
